## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a stable online statistic with clear time/space guarantees using simple data structures.

## Key Concepts

- streaming algorithms
- two-heap median maintenance
- amortized vs worst-case costs
- space efficiency

## Code Example

```javascript
// production-ready sketch of two-heap median tracker
class BinaryHeap {
  constructor(compare){ this.data=[]; this.compare=compare; }
  size(){ return this.data.length; }
  top(){ return this.data[0]; }
  push(x){ this.data.push(x); this._siftUp(this.data.length-1); }
  pop(){ const top=this.data[0]; const end=this.data.pop(); if (this.data.length){ this.data[0]=end; this._siftDown(0); } return top; }
  _siftUp(i){ const x=this.data[i]; while(i>0){ const p=(i-1)>>1; if (this.compare(x, this.data[p])>=0) break; this.data[i]=this.data[p]; i=p; } this.data[i]=x; }
  _siftDown(i){ const n=this.data.length; const x=this.data[i]; while(true){ let l=2*i+1, r=l+1, smallest=i; if (l<n && this.compare(this.data[l], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=l; if (r<n && this.compare(this.data[r], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=r; if (smallest===i) break; this.data[i]=this.data[smallest]; i=smallest; } this.data[i]=x; }
}

class MedianTracker {
  constructor(){ this.lower=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> b-a); this.upper=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> a-b); }
  insert(x){ if (this.lower.size()===0 || x <= this.lower.top()) this.lower.push(x); else this.upper.push(x);
    if (this.lower.size() > this.upper.size()+1) this.upper.push(this.lower.pop());
    else if (this.upper.size() > this.lower.size()) this.lower.push(this.upper.pop());
  }
  median(){ if (this.lower.size() === this.upper.size()) return (this.lower.top() + this.upper.top())/2; return this.lower.top(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for deletions or outliers removal?
- How would you validate correctness with unit tests and random streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming heavy hitters and fixed-memory reasoning. Misra-Gries demonstrates how to bound error while tracking potential mode candidates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries algorithm; heavy-hitters with fixed counters
- Online updates, O(1) amortized per element
- Memory O(k); approximate answer with additive error

## Code Example
```javascript
class MG {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){
    if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1);
    else if(this.map.size < this.k-1) this.map.set(x,1);
    else{ for(const [v,c] of Array.from(this.map)) this.map.set(v,c-1); for(const [v,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(v)<=0) this.map.delete(v);} }
  }
}
```

